        explanation: 'The patient has Pseudohypoparathyroidism Type 1A (PHP1A), also known as Albright Hereditary Osteodystrophy (AHO) with multi-hormone resistance. PHP1A is caused by heterozygous inactivating mutations in the GNAS gene on chromosome 20q13.3, which encodes the alpha subunit of the heterotrimeric stimulatory G-protein (Gs-alpha). Gs-alpha couples membrane receptors for peptide hormones (including PTH, TSH, GHRH, and LH/FSH) to adenylyl cyclase, generating cyclic AMP. GNAS is subject to tissue-specific genomic imprinting: in the renal proximal tubules, thyroid gland, and anterior pituitary, the paternal GNAS allele is epigenetically silenced (methylated), and only the maternal allele is expressed. When an inactivating GNAS mutation is inherited from the mother, the child lacks functional Gs-alpha in these tissues, producing end-organ resistance to PTH (hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and compensatory secondary hyperparathyroidism despite normal kidneys) and mild TSH resistance, along with the AHO physical phenotype (short stature, round face, shortened 4th and 5th metacarpals, subcutaneous calcifications). Conversely, paternal inheritance of the same mutation causes pseudo-pseudohypoparathyroidism (PPHP): the patient has the AHO physical features, but normal calcium and PTH because the maternal allele remains normally active in the kidney.'
