      'Differentiate Familial Hypocalciuric Hypercalcemia (FHH) from Primary Hyperparathyroidism (PHPT): Both present with hypercalcemia and normal or mildly elevated intact PTH. FHH is an autosomal dominant loss-of-function mutation in the CALCIUM-SENSING RECEPTOR (CaSR, Gq-protein coupled) on chromosome 3q. In the parathyroid glands: CaSR requires higher serum calcium to suppress PTH release (calcium set-point shifted rightward). In the kidney (thick ascending limb): defective CaSR increases tubular calcium and magnesium reabsorption. CRUCIAL DECISIVE TEST: 24-HOUR URINE CALCIUM EXCRETION AND CALCIUM-TO-CREATININE CLEARANCE RATIO (CCCR): In FHH, CCCR is < 0.01 (VERY LOW urinary calcium excretion, < 100 mg/day). In Primary Hyperparathyroidism, filtered calcium load overwhelms tubular reabsorption, so urine calcium is ELEVATED (CCCR > 0.02, urine Ca > 200-300 mg/day). FHH is completely BENIGN and ASYMPTOMATIC; parathyroidectomy is INEFFECTIVE and contraindicated.',
