      'Recognize Kallmann Syndrome: genetic disorder caused by defective embryonic migration of GnRH-synthesizing neurons and olfactory placode neurons from the olfactory placode (cribriform plate) to the hypothalamus. Most commonly X-linked recessive (ANOS1 / KAL1 gene encoding anosmin-1) or autosomal dominant (FGFR1 gene). Clinical hallmarks: 1. HYPOGONADOTROPIC HYPOGONADISM (delayed or absent puberty, microphallus and cryptorchidism in infancy, low testosterone, low LH, low FSH); 2. ANOSMIA or HYPOSMIA (complete inability to perceive odors / taste flavors due to hypoplastic or absent olfactory bulbs and tracts). Associated features: bimanual synkinesis (mirror movements of hands), unilateral renal agenesis, high-arched palate. Differentiated from Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) which has HYPERgonadotropic hypogonadism (elevated LH and FSH) and normal olfaction.',
