      'Recognize Craniopharyngioma: most common non-glial pediatric intracranial tumor (bimodal age distribution: children ages 5-14 and adults ages 50-75). Benign, locally aggressive tumor arising from epithelial remnants of RATHKE POUCH (oral ectodermal diverticulum that normally forms the anterior pituitary / adenohypophysis). Located in the SUPRASELLAR region, compressing: 1. OPTIC CHIASM: bitemporal hemianopsia; 2. Pituitary stalk / gland: hypopituitarism (growth failure/short stature from GH deficiency, delayed puberty from gonadotropin deficiency) and DIABETES INSIPIDUS (ADH deficiency from hypothalamic compression); 3. Ventricles: obstructive hydrocephalus and increased ICP (morning headaches, papilledema). Neuroimaging: classic SUPRASELLAR CALCIFIED CYSTIC AND SOLID MASS. Histopathology (adamantinomatous subtype, driven by CTNNB1 / beta-catenin mutations): cords/sheets of stratified squamous epithelium with peripheral palisading, "WET KERATIN" (nodules of ghost/anucleate squames), dystrophic calcifications, and cysts filled with dark, viscous, cholesterol-rich "MOTOR OIL" fluid.',
