        explanation: 'The child has an adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma, the most common childhood suprasellar brain tumor. Craniopharyngiomas are benign, slow-growing, locally destructive epithelial neoplasms that originate from vestigial embryonic remnants of Rathke pouch. During week 4 of embryogenesis, an oral ectodermal diverticulum (Rathke pouch) arises from the roof of the primitive stomodeum and ascends toward the diencephalon to form the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe, pars tuberalis, and pars intermedia), while neuroectoderm from the diencephalic floor descends to form the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary). Incomplete involution leaves ectopic epithelial nests along the craniopharyngeal canal or pituitary stalk. Tumor growth in the suprasellar cistern produces a classic triad: (1) Optic chiasm compression (bitemporal hemianopsia); (2) Hypothalamic/pituitary axis compression (growth hormone failure, delayed puberty, secondary adrenal insufficiency, and central diabetes insipidus); and (3) Increased intracranial pressure (headache, vomiting, papilledema). Neuroimaging reveals a cystic, solid, calcified suprasellar mass. Histopathology demonstrates adamantinomatous features (driven by activating CTNNB1 / beta-catenin mutations): stratified squamous epithelium with peripheral palisading, loose stellate reticulum, dystrophic calcification, compact eosinophilic "wet keratin" pearls, and cystic spaces containing cholesterol-rich "motor-oil" fluid.'
